\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Quadratic isometry forgets the reference pairing and projection}
\date{2026-10-18}

\p{The [enriched classification](fdip-000X) changes if
one keeps only a positive quadratic form and permits every linear
isometry. Any two positive symmetric matrices #{Q_1,Q_2} of the
same size define isometric quadratic spaces, even when their
counting-relative spectra and retained observables differ.}

\p{\citet{Appendix A.5.2, pp. 646-647}{boyd2004convex} gives the
symmetric spectral decomposition and positive square root used below
to construct the isometry.}

\p{For #{Q=O\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_i)O^T}, with #{O}
orthogonal and every #{\lambda_i>0}, define
##{\begin{aligned}
Q^{1/2}&=O\operatorname{diag}(\sqrt{\lambda_i})O^T,\\
A=Q^{-1/2}&=O\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_i^{-1/2})O^T.
\end{aligned}}
Direct multiplication gives #{A^TQA=I}. More generally,
##{\begin{aligned}
T&=Q_2^{-1/2}Q_1^{1/2},\\
T^TQ_2T
 &=Q_1^{1/2}Q_2^{-1/2}Q_2Q_2^{-1/2}Q_1^{1/2}\\
 &=Q_1.
\end{aligned}}
The map #{T} is invertible. This calculation does not require
#{Q_1Q_2=Q_2Q_1}. Thus positive quadratic forms have one
linear-isometry class in each fixed dimension.}

\p{The reference pairing changes under the same substitution
#{\phi=Az}: its matrix becomes
##{H'=A^TIA=Q^{-1},}
while the action form has matrix #{I}. If that transported pairing
is retained, its relative operator is #{(H')^{-1}I=Q}, preserving
the original spectrum. In general simultaneous transport of a positive
reference pairing #{H} and an action form #{Q} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
H'&=A^THA,\qquad Q'=A^TQA,\\
(H')^{-1}Q'&=A^{-1}(H^{-1}Q)A.
\end{aligned}}
Similarity preserves the spectrum. Replacing #{H'} by #{I} after
normalizing #{Q} discards this data; it is not simultaneous transport
of the pair.}

\p{For the four-cycle with #{a=m=1}, compare #{b=1} and #{b=2}.
The [spectral calculation](fdip-0037) gives respectively
##{\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{spec}(Q_1)&=\{1,3,3,5\},\\
\operatorname{spec}(Q_2)&=\{1,3,5,7\}.
\end{aligned}}
These forms are linearly isometric by #{T}, but cannot be conjugate
by a counting-orthogonal map, since their spectra differ. In particular
they are not compatible relabelings. Their
[trace ratios](fdip-000Y) also differ by #{1/72}.}

\p{The same isometry gives a concrete obstruction to the retained
projection, even though it preserves the named half-turn. In the
common orthonormal spectral basis #{u_0,u_1,u_2,u_3}, the matrix
of #{T} is
##{\operatorname{diag}\bigl(1,1,\sqrt{3/5},\sqrt{5/7}\bigr).}
The half-turn is diagonal in this basis as well, so
#{T\rho(h)=\rho(h)T}. Returning to vertex coordinates gives
##{T_{01}=\frac{\sqrt{3/5}-\sqrt{5/7}}4\ne0,}
because #{3/5\ne 5/7}. For #{R=R_0}, one has
#{\pi_R\delta_1=0}, whereas the zeroth component of
#{\pi_RT\delta_1} is nonzero. Hence no linear map #{T_R} can
satisfy #{\pi_RT=T_R\pi_R}. Also #{T^TT} has eigenvalues
#{1,1,3/5,5/7}, so #{T} fails to preserve the counting pairing.
These are separate failures of the enriched-arrow conditions.}

\p{A nonunit Jacobian is not an obstruction to transport of the
normalized Gaussian. If #{T^TQ_2T=Q_1}, then
##{\det Q_1=(\det T)^2\det Q_2.}
For #{y=T\phi}, the exponent in the
[normalized density](fdip-0005) becomes #{-y^TQ_2y/2}; its
coefficient after the [reference change of variables](fdip-002K) is
##{\frac{\sqrt{\det Q_1}}{(2\pi)^{n/2}|\det T|}
 =\frac{\sqrt{\det Q_2}}{(2\pi)^{n/2}}.}
Therefore #{T_*\mu_{Q_1}=\mu_{Q_2}}. Equivalently,
#{Q_2^{-1}=TQ_1^{-1}T^T} is the covariance transport law.
For #{A=Q^{-1/2}}, the generally nonunit Jacobian
#{|\det A|=(\det Q)^{-1/2}} is exactly absorbed by normalization.
The loss is the discarded pairing, carrier and retained projection,
not a failure of normalized probability transport.}

\p{TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{Matrix.PosDef.exists_generalLinearGroup_mul_transpose_eq}
provides an invertible factor #{C} with #{Q=CC^T} for a real
positive-definite matrix on a finite index set. For a bare witness
of that conclusion the normalizer is #{C^{-T}}, since
##{(C^{-T})^TQ C^{-T}=C^{-1}CC^TC^{-T}=I.}
The theorem's proof chooses the symmetric positive square root,
but its existential conclusion does not assert symmetry of #{C}.
One therefore cannot infer that #{C^{-1}} is the normalizer
from the factorization alone. Its separate
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}
supports Gaussian transport in covariance parameters, after the
precision-density identification. Neither declaration supplies the
enriched classification or projection obstruction.}

\p{By [Clifford functoriality](fdip-000Q), an isometry of these
configuration forms also induces an algebra isomorphism.
Since the positive forms already lie in one isometry class, keeping
only the abstract quadratic space, or its isometry-functorial Clifford
algebra, cannot recover the discarded reference pairing, carrier and
projection. This argument does not infer a quadratic isometry from
an arbitrary Clifford-algebra isomorphism. Retaining the enriched
objects and their specified relabeling arrows prevents the demonstrated
collapse; it supplies no continuum or physical selection map.}
